\section*{Introduction}
Modern video games are increasingly viewed as more than entertainment: they are structured, data-rich systems where learning algorithms can be trained, evaluated, and deployed at scale. As DeepMind notes, ``Games have been used for decades as an important way to test and evaluate the performance of artificial intelligence systems.'' \cite{deepmind_alphastar} This project focuses on the rhythm game \textit{osu!} \cite{osu_mode_wiki} and aims to build a deep learning ``map reader'' that segments a beatmap \cite{osu_beatmap_wiki} into labeled patterns. Further application of a successful model can be applied to support beatmap difficulty calculation, which helps maintain the healthiness of the game. Deep learning is a reasonable approach because these patterns are sequential and context-dependent: the same local spacing can represent different gameplay intent depending on surrounding rhythm, angle changes, and slider velocity, making heuristics brittle across different beatmaps.



