\section*{Background \& Related Work}
Recent works motivate learning-based approaches for understanding and evaluating structure in games and, more specifically, rhythm-game charts. TaikoNation targets \emph{patterning-focused} chart generation, showing that neural models can learn and reproduce recurring chart motifs \cite{halina2021taikonation}. Mania Archetype also studies the generation of rhythm-game charts, reinforcing the idea that chart structure can be modeled in a way that reflects player experience \cite{wang2024mania_archetype}. Osu2MIR demonstrates that the information from osu-derived beatmaps can be repurposed into a research dataset \cite{liu2025osu2mir}. More broadly, the PCGML literature surveys deep learning methods for procedural game content, positioning rhythm-game charts as a viable domain for learned structure in game design artifacts \cite{liu2021dl_pcg}. In terms of ML used to evaluate game-related data, esports analytics has applied machine learning to infer results from game signals \cite{garcia2025esports_win_pred}. These works collectively motivate the goal of learning an interpretable pattern labeling of \texttt{.osu} hit-object sequences (Level-1 and Level-2) rather than only producing coarse difficulty summaries.




\section*{Data Processing}
Beatmaps that contain the \texttt{.osu} files can be obtained from osu!'s official beatmap listing \cite{osu_beatmap_listing}. 
\texttt{.osu} files will be parsed according to the structure of \texttt{.osu} files \cite{osu_file_format}. A preprocess program will parse the two relevant sections for this project: \texttt{[TimingPoints]} and \texttt{[HitObjects]}. To parse, subsequent lines are read until the next header line beginning with [ or end-of-file; by iterating through the file line-by-line (e.g., \texttt{for line in f:}) and using the read and write function \cite{so_read_write_open} will be effective. Parsed data will be stored as feature.file (.csv or .txt)

After creating feature.file, the objects will receive a pseudo label from a heuristic program by computing the beat-normalized spatiotemporal relationships between objects (e.g., $\Delta b$, $\Delta x$, $\Delta y$, normalized spacing $d/R$, and turn-angle features).   These quantities, together with a Level-1 label, will be appended to each object entry. They will then be manually corrected and labeled for Level-2 subtypes. The resulting corrected dataset is split into training/validation/test subsets.

The official definition of patterns are incomplete on the osu! wiki \cite{osu_pattern_wiki}. But this Youtube video \cite{osu_pattern_youtube} provides the very basic definitions. This project will have the following categories: 

Level-1: Jumps, Streams, Sliders, Filler \\
Level-2: Jump Aim, Awkward Aim, Flow Aim, Accelerating Flow Aim, Slider Aim, Linear Aim


\section*{Architecture}
\textbf{Transformer encoder} will be used as the main model. Intuitively, a Transformer processes a sequence by using \emph{self-attention}, which allows the prediction at one hit object to depend on other nearby or distant hit objects in the same clip. This is useful because osu! patterns are defined by relationships across multiple notes. The Transformer outputs a representation for each hit object, and then a small classification layer converts these representations into pattern labels.

There are two levels of output implementation: (1) a simple Level-1 classifier, and (2) an Level-2 classifier that brings the label down to subtypes within each Level-1 category. Exact details such as the number of layers, hidden dimension, and attention heads will be chosen experimentally during implementation.




\section*{Baseline Model}
Currently, \texttt{osu!} has a heuristic difficulty evaluation system that assess the notes' spacing and speed, and provides it with a numerical rating, while this project does not have a scope large enough to train a model that provide something at that level of complexity, this existing heuristic can be used as a baseline when the neural network receives further development. This project is merely a baseline for something bigger.


\section*{Ethical Considerations}
Beatmaps may include copyrighted assets (audio/backgrounds), so the pipeline will use only \texttt{.osu} text and derived numeric features, and will not share media files. Since beatmaps are community-created, the project will follow osu!'s content usage permissions \cite{osu_content_usage}. Finally, pattern labels are subjective (e.g., whether the aim is awkward or not), so the annotations may reflect personal bias and the model may inherit it, so labeling criteria and failure cases will be documented; labeling rules and outputs will be presented as descriptive rather than authoritative.





